1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:07,560
Why can paper bills or digits on a phone buy your coffee? Before money, people bartered—trading fish directly for bread!

2
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:15,769
What if the baker hated fish? Humans solved this with commodity money: shells, salt, and shiny gold coins everyone valued.

3
00:00:16,150 --> 00:00:23,625
Hauling heavy gold was exhausting! So goldsmiths locked it in vaults and issued paper receipts—the very first banknotes.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,964
Today we use fiat money, which isn't backed by gold at all. It works because of shared trust and law.

5
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:36,613
In fact, over ninety percent of money is digital! Banks lend out your deposits to help build homes and businesses.

6
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:44,499
But when too much money chases too few goods, prices balloon! That is inflation—making every dollar buy less.

7
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:51,580
Central banks act like an economic thermostat—raising interest rates to cool inflation, or cutting them to spark jobs.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:59,706
Money isn't just metal, paper, or pixels. It is humanity's greatest invention for cooperation—a shared story of trust!
